{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "def is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the digits of the given number strictly alternate between increasing and decreasing.\n    A number with fewer than 3 digits cannot be undulating by this definition.\n    \"\"\"\n    s = str(abs(number))\n    n = len(s)\n\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    digits = [int(d) for d in s]\n\n    # Find the first pair of distinct adjacent digits to establish the initial direction.\n    initial_direction_set = False\n    expected_increase = False\n    k = 0\n    while k < n - 1:\n        if digits[k] != digits[k+1]:\n            expected_increase = (digits[k] < digits[k+1])\n            initial_direction_set = True\n            break\n        k += 1\n\n    # If no distinct adjacent digits were found (e.g., 111, 2222), it's not undulating.\n    if not initial_direction_set:\n        return False\n\n    # Check the alternation pattern from the point where the initial direction was set.\n    for j in range(k, n - 1):\n        d1 = digits[j]\n        d2 = digits[j+1]\n\n        # Digits must be strictly alternating; identical adjacent digits mean it's not undulating.\n        if d1 == d2:\n            return False\n\n        if expected_increase:\n            # We expect d1 < d2\n            if d1 >= d2:\n                return False\n        else:\n            # We expect d1 > d2\n            if d1 <= d2:\n                return False\n\n        # Flip the expected direction for the next pair.\n        expected_increase = not expected_increase\n\n    return True"}
